---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const valueCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'value = benefits − costs', note: 'The opening move of any marketing course (MIT OCW 15.810 begins here): a customer buys nothing but value. Marketing is the discipline of creating, presenting and delivering that value — "meeting needs profitably" (Kotler & Keller).' },
    { math: 'perceived benefits = B', note: 'Everything the customer gains, as they perceive it: the product\'s performance, the time and hassle it saves, the status or reassurance it carries. Note the word perceived — a benefit the customer does not perceive is worth zero, no matter how real it is in the lab.' },
    { math: 'perceived costs = C = price + effort + risk', note: 'Everything the customer gives up: the money (price), the search and learning effort, and the risk of being wrong. Cost is broader than the price tag, which is why a cheap product can still be poor value.' },
    { math: 'worked example: B = 35, C = 18', note: 'A streaming subscription: entertainment value ≈ 28 plus convenience ≈ 7, so B = 35. Price ≈ 15 plus setup/effort ≈ 3, so C = 18. Customer value = 35 − 18 = 17 > 0 — the deal beats the status quo, so the exchange happens.' },
    { math: 'two levers: raise B or cut C', note: 'Every strategy in the course is a move on this equation. Premium positioning raises B; a lower price cuts C; better distribution and clearer communication cut the effort and risk terms. Competitors enter by offering a larger value gap than yours.' },
  ],
};

const valueLab = {
  params: [
    { name: 'benefits', label: 'perceived benefits', min: 0, max: 100, step: 1, value: 60, unit: 'pts' },
    { name: 'price', label: 'perceived price', min: 0, max: 100, step: 1, value: 25, unit: 'pts' },
    { name: 'switching', label: 'switching cost', min: 0, max: 60, step: 1, value: 10, unit: 'pts' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'net customer value', expr: 'benefits - price - switching', unit: 'pts' },
    { label: 'value ratio (B/C)', expr: 'benefits / max(price + switching, 1)', unit: 'x' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/marketing/foundational/introduction-and-company-analysis">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    MIT's 15.810 <em>Marketing Management</em> opens with a definition students routinely
    get wrong: marketing is <strong>not advertising</strong>. It is the process of
    <em>creating, presenting and delivering value</em> to customers — and of capturing value
    back for the firm, profitably. Kotler and Keller compress it to "meeting needs
    profitably". Everything in the course — product policy, pricing, promotion, channels —
    is a lever on that one sentence. And the analysis always starts at home: what can this
    company actually do, and what do its competitors already offer the same customer?
  </p>
  <p>
    The definition deserves unpacking because each clause kills a misconception. "Creating"
    value: marketing begins before the product exists, in choosing which needs to serve —
    the discipline is upstream of engineering. "Presenting": the same value poorly
    communicated is worth less, so perception is part of the product, not a spin on it.
    "Delivering": value that never reaches the customer conveniently is not delivered —
    distribution is marketing. And "capturing value back, profitably": the firm must keep
    enough of the value it creates to survive, which is why marketing always arrives holding
    hands with finance. A company that creates value but captures none (a free service with
    no path to revenue) is a charity; one that captures value without creating it (a monopoly
    extract) is in trouble the moment competition arrives. The subject's whole logic lives
    between those two failure modes.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> marketing is selling — persuading people to want things
  they don't need. Drucker's classic correction: the aim of marketing is to make selling
  superfluous, by understanding the customer so well that the product fits and sells itself.
  Selling starts after the product exists; marketing starts before, and its best work is
  invisible.</p>

  <h2>The exchange, and the value equation at its core</h2>
  <p>
    A market is built on <strong>exchange</strong>: two parties each give up something they
    value less for something they value more. Exchange happens only when the customer's
    perceived value is positive — and preferably larger than any alternative's. That single
    condition, <em>value = perceived benefits − perceived costs</em>, is the most useful
    model in the subject. Walk it through step by step:
  </p>
  <Widget no="1" caption="customer value built step by step, with a worked example" widget="equationStep" config={valueCfg} />
  <p>
    Three features of the equation repay attention. First, everything is <em>perceived</em>:
    a benefit the customer doesn't notice is worth zero, and a risk they don't perceive
    doesn't cost them (until it does — then it costs your brand). This is why communication
    is a value lever and not decoration: making a real benefit visible raises B as surely as
    improving the product does. Second, the customer compares offers on <em>net</em> value,
    so the status quo is always your rival: "do nothing" has benefits (no cost, no risk) and
    every innovation must beat it. Third, the equation explains segmentation before you have
    even met the term: different customers weight the terms differently — the commuter
    weighs time (effort), the student weighs price, the enterprise buyer weighs risk — so
    the same offer has different values to different people, and a firm can aim itself at
    the segment where its gap is largest.
  </p>

  <h2>Company analysis: what can this firm actually do?</h2>
  <p>
    Before choosing a strategy you map the playing field — the course's "3C" frame:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Company:</strong> what can we do better or cheaper than anyone? Assets, capabilities, cost structure. Strategy that ignores capability is a wish.</li>
    <li><strong>Customers:</strong> what do they need, how do they perceive value, how do they differ from each other? Benefits and costs live in the customer's perception, not in the spec sheet.</li>
    <li><strong>Competitors:</strong> who else is bidding for the same value? A value gap of +17 is only attractive if the rival offer isn't +25. Positioning is always relative.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Company analysis is the C of the three, and it is more than listing strengths. The
    analytical questions are: what is our <strong>cost structure</strong> (can we afford to
    compete on price, or must we compete on benefits)? What are our <strong>capabilities and
    assets</strong> — technology, brand, distribution, data, people — and are they things
    competitors can buy, or advantages that compound? Strategy frameworks formalise this as
    a sustainability test (the VRIO questions): is the resource <em>Valuable</em>, <em>Rare</em>,
    hard to <em>Imitate</em>, and is the firm <em>Organised</em> to exploit it? A strength
    that passes all four is a durable advantage; one that fails is a feature. A café's
    excellent barista is valuable but imitable — she can be hired away; a logistics network
    or a brand built over decades is not. Company analysis exists to force that honesty
    before the strategy is written.
  </p>
  <p>
    From the 3Cs, Porter's insight follows: durable profit comes from a structural position
    competitors cannot easily copy, not from merely having a good product this year.
  </p>

  <h2>Competitive analysis: mapping the contest</h2>
  <p>
    Value is always judged <em>against alternatives</em> — the customer's reference point is
    the next-best offer plus the status quo of doing nothing. That is why company analysis
    alone is never enough: an offer can improve every year and still lose share, because a
    competitor's offer improved faster. The competitor audit therefore asks three questions
    — <strong>who</strong> are our direct and indirect competitors (the indirect half is
    where audits fail: the cinema competes with every streaming subscription, the city
    hotel with the videoconference), <strong>what value</strong> do they deliver (not their
    feature lists — their value gaps for each segment), and <strong>where are we stronger or
    weaker, and why</strong>? The audit ends in a positioning map: customers' two most
    decisive attributes as axes (say price against convenience), competitors plotted as
    points, and the empty space — the segment nobody serves well — as the strategic prize.
  </p>
  <p>
    Porter's <strong>five forces</strong> deepen the map from rivals to structure. The
    intensity of rivalry is only one force: the threat of <em>new entrants</em> (how easy is
    it to enter?), the threat of <em>substitutes</em> (adjacent solutions to the same need),
    the <em>bargaining power of suppliers</em> (can they squeeze your margin?) and the
    <em>bargaining power of buyers</em> (can they squeeze yours?) complete the picture. An
    industry can have polite rivals and still be brutal if buyers are concentrated and
    entry is cheap (contract manufacturing); or vicious rivals with a protected position
    (two mobile ecosystems). The forces tell you where profit goes — and therefore which
    strategic position is worth building.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> competing means being better than every competitor at
  everything. The generic strategies say otherwise: a firm that tries to be best-at-
  everything usually achieves lowest-at-something, because the strategies have different
  cost structures — broad cost leadership, focused differentiation, niche specialisation.
  The strategic sin Porter named "stuck in the middle" is not choosing.</p>

  <h2>Play with the value gap</h2>
  <Widget no="2" caption="slide benefits, price and switching cost — watch net value and the value ratio respond" widget="sliderLab" config={valueLab} />
  <p>
    Two readouts matter. <em>Net value</em> (benefits − price − switching cost) must exceed
    the customer's best alternative or no exchange happens; <em>the value ratio</em>
    (benefits per unit of total cost) shows why "expensive but excellent" can beat "cheap
    but mediocre" — a ratio of 4× can coexist with a small absolute margin. Notice how
    switching cost quietly subtracts from value while protecting the incumbent: the same
    number appears in both the customer's decision and the firm's moat. That double
    appearance is the bridge between the two halves of this lesson — a switching cost is a
    company asset (raising the incumbent's C-to-leave) that shows up in the customer's
    equation as a cost, and firms spend real money (loyalty points, ecosystems, default
    settings) to build it.
  </p>

  <h2>Where this course goes next</h2>
  <p>
    With value and the 3Cs established, 15.810 turns to the classic sequence: segment the
    market into groups with distinct needs, target the segment(s) the firm can serve best,
    and position the offering in the customer's mind (STP). Then the tactical quartet —
    product, price, promotion, place — becomes a set of coordinated bets on the same value
    equation you just played with.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked miniature ties the pieces together. Two campus coffee shops sell identical
    beans. One competes on price (cutting C), the other on speed and a study-friendly room
    (raising B for the between-lectures segment). Neither is "the best coffee" — each has
    chosen a segment and built a coherent value gap for it. The analysis behind the second
    one reads exactly like this lesson: company (small space, good atmosphere, no economies
    of scale — price war unwinnable), customers (students: time-poor between lectures,
    space-hungry during study hours), competitors (the chains: cheaper, faster lines, worse
    seating) — and from those three lists, the position follows almost mechanically. That is
    the whole course in one block: analysis first, position second, tactics third — and
    every tactic traceable back to a number in the value equation.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three refinements belong on the expert layer. First, <strong>value capture vs value
    creation</strong>: the value equation says nothing about who keeps the surplus. In
    competitive markets, customer value gets competed away into price cuts; firms with
    switching costs, brands or network effects keep more of it — which is why the industry
    analysis (five forces) and the value equation must be read together to predict
    profitability. Second, <strong>dynamic vs static</strong>: the audit is a snapshot, but
    markets move — entrants arrive, needs migrate, technology resets switching costs to
    zero (the smartphone erased the mobile-operator's lock-in overnight). Good company
    analysis therefore includes a view of <em>where the industry is heading</em>, not just
    where it stands; the classic failure is optimising for a position the market is leaving.
    Third, <strong>measurement honesty</strong>: "perceived benefits" cannot be read off a
    spreadsheet — they are estimated through customer research (surveys, conjoint analysis
    that trades features against price, A/B tests) and revised continuously. The most
    common fatal error in company analysis is not a wrong framework; it is filling the
    frameworks with what the firm wishes were true rather than what customers would tell
    you. Marketing's empirical habits — test, measure, revise — exist precisely because
    perception, the equation's central quantity, can only be observed in the customer.
  </p>
  <p>
    A final integrating exercise rehearses the whole lesson on one firm, start to finish.
    Take a mid-size meal-kit delivery company. <em>Value equation:</em> benefits are
    convenience, reduced food waste, novelty of recipes (perceived B); costs are the
    subscription price, the cooking time still required, and the lock-in anxiety of a weekly
    box (perceived C). <em>Company analysis:</em> its supply chain is genuinely hard to copy
    (cold logistics, portioning, recipe IP) but its product is easily copied in concept — a
    VRIO split verdict that argues for competing on operations, not features.
    <em>Competitive analysis:</em> direct rivals are other kits; indirect rivals are
    supermarket ready-meals, takeaway apps and — the status quo every kit must beat — the
    grocery run itself; the five-forces view says buyer power is high (switching cost near
    zero, churn by click) and substitutes crowd the field, so profit lives in retention, not
    acquisition. <em>Positioning implication:</em> the firm should spend its marketing on the
    segment with the largest value gap — time-poor households who currently <em>waste</em>
    food (raising their B by naming the waste saved) — rather than on price-cutting that
    cannibalises a margin it cannot defend. Notice what the exercise demonstrates about the
    discipline: no framework produced the answer alone; the answer is what remains after the
    frameworks have each narrowed the space — which is exactly why the course teaches
    analysis before creativity.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> MIT OpenCourseWare 15.810 Marketing Management (course overview and calendar); Kotler &amp; Keller, Marketing Management (15th ed.), ch. 1–2; Porter, Competitive Strategy (1980); Barney, "Firm Resources and Sustained Competitive Advantage" (1991).</p>
</TopicPage>
